 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Advancing Front Isosurface Extraction with Guidance Field](947-advancing-front-isosurface-extraction-with-guidance-field.md)
-Uses a scalar guidance field $g(\mathbf{x})$ to constrain edge lengths based on curvature, ensuring robust front interference detection and well-shaped triangles. It replaces heuristic sizing with a theoretically grounded approach. Choose this variant when curvature-adaptive element sizing is required over the base framework's heuristic approach.
+Uses a guidance field to constrain edge lengths based on curvature, solving front interference and poor triangle shaping.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-Not given in the sources.
+*   **Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:** Capable of generating high-quality meshes for general implicit surfaces without predefined volume descriptions. Handles complex topology through front splitting and merging.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires non-zero gradient and Hessian everywhere. Front intersection detection can be computationally expensive. Lacks a theoretical foundation for edge size selection in the base framework, relying on heuristics.
+*   **When to use:** When extracting meshes from general implicit functions where topological changes (splitting/merging) are expected and high-quality element shapes are required.
 
 ## Background
-The advancing front technique originated in the mesh generation community for numerical simulation, where it was used to create high-quality meshes for well-defined domains [S1]. The application to implicit surfaces, where no pre-defined volume description exists, was a significant extension [S1]. Key references include work by Borouchaki et al. on gradation control and other early works on implicit surface triangulation [S1].
+Advancing front algorithms, also known as surface tracking and continuation, have a substantial history in mesh generation for numerical simulation [S1]. In that context, algorithms start with well-defined domain representations and increase dimension (edges, triangles, tetrahedra) [S1]. The application to implicit surfaces, as described in [S1], addresses the challenge of operating without a well-defined description of volume components, requiring chart creation for the reconstructed surface. Previous works in this area include [8, 23, 24] for implicit surface triangulation and [7, 46] for general mesh generation.
 
